<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jul 8, 2022">
<CPscript title="Harmonic Mapping Trials" screenmode="map" >
<description>We display the images of some discrete 'harmonic' mappings of the form h+conj(g).</description>
<text> The notion of "discrete harmonic function" arose in the experimental context of CirclePack. Conversations with Michael Dorff concerning the material in Chapters 2 and 4 of the book led to programming of the 'h_g_bar' command and to a few elementary initial experiments. I hope engaged users can carry development forward. </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" tooltip="Restart" iconname="userconfig.png">act 0;Map 0 1;Cleanse;infile_read dom.p;color -c 20 b;Disp -w -c -cf b;copy 1;act 1;color -c 20 b;Disp -w -c -cf b </cmd>
<text> Please review the script "HGintro.xmd" to see the basic experimental apparatus. CirclePack is not an ideal environment --- there are too many functions being juggled --- so our setup is rather rigid:

(1) Discrete analytic functions h and g always have their ranges in p0 and p1, respectively. They share the same maximal packing as domain. (The user should feel free to replace the 'dom.p' file in the initial '*' command.)

(2) f = h + conj(g) always appears in p2. Press '1' to see h and g, '2' to see g and f.

(3) h will generally be univalent; it starts out, in fact, as the identity. </text>
<Section title="Blaschke Dilatations">
<text> Harmonic mappings whose dilatations are Blaschke products play important roles in Chapters 4 and 5 of the book. In this section we describe tools to look at the discrete analogue. 

The requirement is that packings for h and g share boundary radii and that any branch points of h are also branch points (of the same or greater multiplicity) of g. The tools used in this section preserve these properties. 

In the following commands we: * reinitialize h and g;  * add branch points to g; * increase some boundary radii (of both) and decrease others; * rotate g by pi/3; * change the 'alpha' vertex of g; and finally, * color code p2 by quasiconformal distortion (of the map from the domain); * add branching at the same vertex of both both h and g. Tools for such actions are described at the end of the section so you can try them in other ways. </text>
<cmd tooltip="Restart first Section" iconname="metacontact_online.png">max_pack -p0;geom_to_e -p0;set_screen -p0 -d;Disp -p0 -w -c -cf b;copy -p0 1;set_screen -p1 -d;Disp -p1 -w -c -cf b;[f];[1] </cmd>
<cmd iconname="icq_dnd.png">set_aim -p1 4.0 13 33;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f];[2] </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kuickshow.png">adjust_rad -p0 .5 b(97 103);adjust_rad -p0 1.5 b(115 109);repack -p0;layout -p0;disp -p0 -wr;adjust_rad -p1 .5 b(97 103);adjust_rad -p1 1.5 b(115 109);repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f];[2]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="mozilla.png">rotate -p1 .333333;[f];[2]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">alpha -p1 10;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f];[2]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">[k]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">set_aim -p0 4.0 10;repack -p0;layout -p0;disp -p0 -wr;set_aim -p1 4.0 10;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f];[2] </cmd>
<text> The following tools for these operations are provided in the "Tools" Section. (We keep h univalent simply so that we can use the mouse in p0 to select for these tools.)

* Press 'f' to apply 'h_g_bar', putting the result in p2.

* Press 'b' on an interior circle of p0 to make it a branch point in p1.

* Press 'B' on an interior circle of p0 to make it a branch point in BOTH p0 and p1.

* Press 'x' to remove all branching from p0 and p1.

* Press 'i' (resp. 'd') on a boundary circle of p0 or p1 to increase (resp. decrease) that radius by 25 percent. (You're welcome to adjust the magnitude of the changes.)

* Press 'r' to rotate p1 by pi/3.

* Press 'a' on an interior circle of p0 to make it become the 'alpha' vertex of p1.

* Press 'k' to color code p2 by quasiconformal distortion. This compares the (eucl) faces of p2 to p0. The most distorted by blue. (Note that this overrides the blue that was used to show reverse orientation.) </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Additional Ideas">
<text> Remove the linkage between h and g (common boundary radii and branching) will bring out additional phenomena. The tools described earlier will continue to work, but we add the tools 'l' (resp. 's') which make all the boundary radii of p1 20 percent larger (resp. smaller), leaving those of p0 unchanged. The following commands restart, add branching to p1, then apply 'l' several times. Study the image; the blue faces are those that are reverse oriented in p2. What does the image suggest? (Hint: look in Chapter 2.) </text>
<cmd tooltip="Restart Second Section" iconname="format_increaseindent.png">max_pack -p0;geom_to_e -p0;set_screen -p0 -d;Disp -p0 -w -c -cf b;copy -p0 1;set_screen -p1 -d;Disp -p1 -w -c -cf b;[f];[1] </cmd>
<cmd iconname="icq_dnd.png">set_aim -p1 4.0 9 16;rld -p1;[f];[2]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="editdelete.png">[l];[l];[l];[l];[l] </cmd>
<text> Remove the linkage between h and g (common boundary radii and branching) will bring out additional phenomena. The tools described earlier will continue to work, but we add the tools 'l' (resp. 's') which make all the boundary radii of p1 10 percent larger (resp. smaller), leaving those of p0 unchanged. Look at these images a while. What do they suggest? (Hint: look in Chapter 2.)

Of course, you can make any other changes you want using the normal command structure. Building additional tools is often helpful since you can then start over and repeat your experiments more easily. Remember that when you make changes to p0 or p1 you will typically have to repack, layout, and redisplay and invoke 'f' to get the image in p2.

Discrete harmonic functions is a wide open area. I hope you enjoy not only playing with it, but thinking about what's going on. If you get deeply engaged and have some Java experience, this is a topic that deserves a 'PackExtender' class to make experiments easier. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="1" mnemonic="1" tooltip="Show h and g" iconname="amarok.png">Map 0 1; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="2" mnemonic="2" tooltip="Show g and f" iconname="apollon.png">Map 1 2 </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="a" mnemonic="a" tooltip="choose an alpha circle" iconname="run.png">set_Vlist z #XY;alpha -p0 Vlist[0];alpha -p1 Vlist[0];disp -p0 -wr;repack -p0;layout -p0;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="b" mnemonic="b" tooltip="Designate a branch point for g" iconname="mozilla.png">set_vlist z #XY;set_Vlist vlist;set_aim -p1 4.0 Vlist;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="B" mnemonic="B" tooltip="set a common branch point for both h and g" iconname="amarok.png">set_vlist z #XY;set_Vlist vlist;set_aim -p0 4.0 Vlist;repack -p0;layout -p0;disp -p0 -wr;set_aim -p1 4.0 Vlist;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="x" mnemonic="x" tooltip="clear all branch points" iconname="metacontact_offline.png">set_aim -p0 -d;rld -p0;set_aim -p1 -d;rld -p1;[f]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="c" mnemonic="c" tooltip="display the circles of p2" iconname="metacontact_online.png">disp -p2 -c; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="k" mnemonic="k" tooltip="Color code the quasiconformal distortion of the faces of p2" iconname="icq_dnd.png">color -p2 -f qc -q0 -m 5.0;disp -p2 -w -ff; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="d" mnemonic="d" tooltip="decrease a boundary circle" iconname="bookmark_add.png">set_Vlist z #XY;adjust_rad -p0 .75 Vlist;repack -p0;layout -p0;disp -p0 -w -c -cf b;put_data -p0 -q1 -r b;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f] </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="i" mnemonic="i" tooltip="increase a boundary circle" iconname="editdelete.png">set_Vlist z #XY;adjust_rad -p0 1.25 Vlist;repack -p0;layout -p0;disp -p0 -w -c -cf b;put_data -p0 -q1 -r b;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f] </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="r" mnemonic="r" tooltip="rotate g" iconname="kghostview.png">rotate -p1 .3333333;disp -p1 -wr;[f]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="l" mnemonic="l" tooltip="increase radii of g" iconname="userconfig.png">adjust_rad -p1 1.2 b;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f] </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="s" mnemonic="s" tooltip="decrease the radii of g" iconname="metacontact_online.png">adjust_rad -p1 .8 b;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -wr;[f] </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="f" mnemonic="f" tooltip="apply h_g_bar, results in p2" iconname="bookmark_add.png">act 2;h_g_bar;color -f 20 j;Disp -w -f -ffc j; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
